#: ../../source/docs/training-mode.rst:5
#: 6b66a006c775475e925e801e3be7bc0c
msgid "TRAINING Mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/training-mode.rst:7
#: 56e558d54e504e768871e0895de35c3c
msgid "TRAINING mode is ideal for teaching students manual R/C control. It gives users complete control over the rudder and throttle, but clips the maximum roll and maximum/minimum pitch to certain limits which cannot be exceeded. TRAINING mode also restricts plane roll to the :ref:`Stall Prevention <stall-prevention>` roll limits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/training-mode.rst:13
#: 2f176fb69edd452885f5c26460a94cac
msgid "More specifically:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/training-mode.rst:15
#: 264d7d2c68284e60a91e60bee7b5ebbf
msgid "If the roll is less the :ref:`ROLL_LIMIT_DEG <ROLL_LIMIT_DEG>` parameter than the pilot has manual roll control. If the plane tries to roll past that limit then the roll will be held at that limit. The plane will not automatically roll back to level flight, but it will prevent the pilot from rolling past the limit. The same applies to pitch - the pilot has manual pitch control until the :ref:`PTCH_LIM_MIN_DEG <PTCH_LIM_MIN_DEG>` or :ref:`PTCH_LIM_MAX_DEG <PTCH_LIM_MAX_DEG>` limits are reached, at which point the plane won't allow the pitch to go past those limits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/training-mode.rst:24
#: 837fed3551354f4b9dc5cbac380f31e2
msgid "When turning, the autopilot will monitor the demanded bank angle and airspeed and work out if there is a sufficient margin above the stall speed to turn at the demanded bank angle. If not the bank angle is limited to the safe value. The stall prevention system will always allow a bank of at least 25 degrees (to ensure you can still manoeuvre if your airspeed estimate is badly off)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/training-mode.rst:30
#: c3a114e67e8b48f192dbdd1e98fc0725
msgid "The rudder and throttle are both completely under manual control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/training-mode.rst:32
#: 3ad85dbf9a5e4022854f22c45f289479
msgid "the :ref:`MAN_EXPO_ROLL<MAN_EXPO_ROLL>`, :ref:`MAN_EXPO_PITCH<MAN_EXPO_PITCH>`, and :ref:`MAN_EXPO_RUDDER<MAN_EXPO_RUDDER>` parameters will apply exponential to the stick inputs, if non-zero, in this mode. This is for users with transmitters which do not provide this function and desire to \"soften\" stick feel around neutral."
msgstr ""
